**Done — the "Upload Photo" text now carries a looping metallic shine, clipped to the glyphs only. Everything else about the button is untouched.**

**How it works:** the text node is wrapped in a dedicated span (`.dashboard-diagnosis-upload-text`). A narrow white/silver band — soft edges, bright `0.98` core at a slight 100° angle — is painted as a gradient on that span and clipped to the letterforms with `background-clip: text` + `text-fill-color: transparent`. Because the gradient's flat sections are `currentColor`, the text keeps its **exact existing color everywhere outside the band**, in both light and dark themes automatically. The gradient layer is oversized (`260%` width) so the band can travel the full text and rest fully off-glyph.

**The loop (measured by scrubbing the animation):** `background-position 96% → 46.2% → 1.7%` across the first 42% of the cycle — the visible left→right sweep over ~1.9s — then the band parks off-glyph for the remaining ~2.6s (a natural pause between passes), and the reset happens entirely while the band is outside the text, so there is **no visible jump**. Total loop 4.5s, `linear` during the sweep, infinite.

**Verified untouched (computed, live):** button border (1px, unchanged in both themes), button background, height 42px, pill radius, the Upload icon (`svg` intact), click → file-picker functionality, font/size/weight/position of the text, Crop Diagnosis layout, and Take Photo's chrome ring. The shine exists only as background pixels inside the text glyphs — no glow, no box-shadow, nothing on the border or background. `prefers-reduced-motion` disables the sweep, leaving the normal static text. Production build passes.

One honest note, as with earlier animations in this environment: the webview's timeline throttles under probing (it reported `running` but frozen), so I proved the sweep by scrubbing the animation's `currentTime` directly — the position values above are real rendered outputs. In a normal browser it will play continuously. Dials if you want to tune by eye: band brightness (the two white alpha stops), sweep share of the loop (the `42%` keyframe), and total cycle (the `4.5s`).
