Done — the performance pass is complete, verified live, and the build is clean. Here's what the audit actually found and what I changed.

**The one finding that mattered most.** The dark theme's aurora background ran a 22s infinite loop animating `filter: hue-rotate()` **and** `background-position` on a fixed, full-viewport layer. That's a full-screen repaint every frame — but the real damage was indirect: that layer sits *behind* every glass card, the sticky header, and the bottom nav. A backdrop that changes every frame invalidates the browser's `backdrop-filter` cache, so **every translucent surface on screen re-blurred on every single frame**, including during scrolling. The aurora is now static (byte-identical gradients, one layer instead of two — I folded the old `body::after` vignette into the same stacked background, same paint order). Zero full-screen repaints per frame, and every card's blur result is now reusable.

**Scrolling.** The full-viewport blurs got the treatment they needed: the sticky header (24px → 16px, denser tint — it re-blurs on *every* scroll frame because content moves behind it), the floating bottom nav and the sticky AI chat bar (same, via a new documented `--chrome-blur` token), and the full-screen modal backdrops (9–16px → 6–10px with a proportionally denser dim, imperceptible because the dialog in front owns the actual glass). Dialog glass, card glass, and every gradient are untouched.

**Animations now run on the compositor.** The upload-arrow pop was animating `top` (layout every frame, with `will-change: top` asking the browser to keep a layer warm for a property that can't be composited) — it's now `translateY` with the identical −5px/+1.5px deltas, sampled live at each keyframe phase. The voice-mode halo was an infinite `box-shadow` animation — now an overlay whose `opacity` animates. A dead `.dashboard-diagnosis-scanline` rule and its infinite keyframes (referenced by no component) are gone.

**React work.** The dashboard's nine staggered reveal sections were driven by framer-motion writing styles every frame; they're now a pure CSS animation with the same curve and 60ms stagger — and framer-motion verifiably has **zero** presence in the eager bundle. The condition-text auto-fit loop shrank type 0.5px at a time re-reading `scrollWidth` after each write (**up to 40 forced layouts** per pass, re-run on every weather/language change and parent resize) — now 2 measurements via a proportional estimate, plus a ResizeObserver guard that ignores passes where the width didn't change. The notification panel measured `getBoundingClientRect()` **during render**; it's measured once, on the click that opens it. Weather/voice/theme context values are memoized (voice interim transcripts no longer cascade app-wide), `Header`/`VoiceModeCard`/`MarketCard` are memoized so weather arriving can't re-render the market or crop-diagnosis subtrees, and `Layout`'s drawer handlers are stable.

**Loading.** One 1.35MB bundle became a 599KB shell (171KB gzip) that statically imports exactly one thing — the i18n data. 13 routes, the one recharts surface on the dashboard (328KB charting lib now loads on demand, not on first paint), and the `AnimatedNumber` tween (hand-rolled cubic-bezier, no library) all split out; eager CSS went 184KB → 99KB. Long card grids got `content-visibility: auto` with `auto` intrinsic sizing so the scrollbar can't jump, and every card image now decodes asynchronously.

