## Root cause (this was a stacking bug, not missing markup)

The icons never disappeared from the DOM — the previous round's icon-contrast fix confirmed them present and styled. What changed was the *paint order consequence* of making the blur real: the mail/lock icons are absolutely-positioned **siblings rendered before the input**, with both elements at `z-index: auto` — so the icons painted *beneath* the input. While the input's `backdrop-filter` was inert (the backdrop-root/transform traps from last round), that ordering was invisible. Once the blur genuinely started sampling the backdrop, it sampled **the icons themselves** and diffused them into the frost. The eye toggle survived only because it comes *after* the input in the DOM; the Google logo was never at risk because it's the button's own child content.

## The fix — two `z-index: 1` declarations, nothing else

`.auth-field__icon` and `.auth-field__toggle` are lifted above the input in stacking order. This matches your required layering concept exactly: glass/blurred backdrop at the bottom → content (icon, text, eye) on top. No blur value, tint, border, radius, dimension, or padding was touched — the input keeps `padding-left: 42px` so text still clears the icon.

