Centering the incomplete last row with a plain grid is awkward. Options:
1. Flexbox with wrap + justify-content: center — this centers every row including incomplete ones, and boxes keep intrinsic width. But then complete rows: user wants side-by-side fill — with flex center, rows are centered but boxes have fixed/max width — they won't stretch to fill the row. Example shows equal-ish boxes in a row; centered last row. Flex wrap centered gives: each row's items centered — complete rows will also be centered (not left-aligned stretched). Hmm, requirement 6 only says final row centered; full rows presumably left-aligned or full-width? The example shows full rows spanning similar width. With flex + center and a consistent box min-width, full rows will look tidy (boxes aligned), last partial row centered. That matches the visual example well.

2. Grid with `grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(160px, 1fr))` + justify-items with last-row centering via `place-items` — CSS can't easily center only the last row. There's a trick: `justify-content: center` on the grid container only centers tracks, not per-row. Actually with `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min 1fr))`, tracks fill the width; last row items sit in their track columns left-aligned. Centering the last incomplete row in pure CSS grid isn't possible without JS.

